598, 2462, 1921)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Traditional Chinese Medicine concepts contextualize liver-related symptoms\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The TCM pattern '肝胆湿热' (damp-heat in liver and gallbladder) provides a diagnostic framework that links symptoms such as flank pain (胁痛) and jaundice. This pattern is used to describe conditions involving biliary and hepatic inflammation, aligning with biomedical understandings of drug-induced liver injury. The inclusion of this TCM entity suggests integration of traditional diagnostic categories with modern pharmacovigilance data, highlighting how cultural medical frameworks may influence symptom reporting and interpretation [Data: Relationships (18487); Entities (1921, 18391, 18598)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Herbal medicine Ye Xiazhu (Phyllanthus) modulates bilirubin metabolism\",\n            \"explanation\": \"叶下珠 (Ye Xiazhu or Phyllanthus) is an herbal agent used in treating jaundice. Research indicates its ethanol extract enhances bilirubin clearance in rats by inducing CAR (constitutive androstane receptor) and CYP3A1 expression, thereby promoting detoxification pathways. This positions Ye Xiazhu as a potentially hepatoprotective agent within the community, contrasting with the hepatotoxic drugs also present. Its mechanism involves upregulation of enzymes involved in bilirubin conjugation and excretion [Data: Relationships (1288); Entities (4258, 18598)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Digestive abnormalities serve as early warning signs of drug-induced liver injury\",\n            \"explanation\": \"消化异常 (digestive abnormalities), including nausea and vomiting, are identified as early symptoms of potential liver damage from drug interactions. These gastrointestinal symptoms often precede more severe manifestations like jaundice and are part of a recognized set of 'warning signals' for adverse drug reactions. Timely recognition of 